Implement a complete FreeRTOS port of dwm3001cdk-lock

Done: assessed feasibility, saved the staged plan, created the sibling application and standalone nRF52833 FreeRTOS port trees, implemented the timing, allocation, mutex, logging, work, semaphore, init-hook, static-thread, serialized OpenThread runtime, static NimBLE/SDC HCI transport, shared MPSL worker, fixed peripheral map, nRF 802.15.4 IRQ/random/temperature contracts, and the MPSL/SoftDevice Controller startup sequencer that claims the frozen interrupt priorities, links only the peripheral GATT/CoC feature set, sizes the static controller pool, registers the entropy source, and publishes the HCI transport contract; ported the pinned NCS opcode dispatcher by compiling it unmodified against a Zephyr-free compat layer and wiring both its command and event halves into the sequencer; brought up the NimBLE host and its FreeRTOS porting layer on a static host task with scheduled controller synchronization and reset recovery, and selected the peripheral-only NimBLE configuration that matches the linked controller features, and implemented the nRF 802.15.4 clock platform on MPSL's source-selecting clock API plus the RTC2 low-power timer with a 64-bit counter extension, compare-race handling, and the timestamper sync channel, and pinned Nordic's Zephyr-free high-precision timer platform for direct compilation; pinned the Qorvo/OpenThread/Nordic/NimBLE source set, and added host, source, and port-purity gates.
Also implemented the persistent key-value store: an append-only log over the two flash pages at 0x7e000 with newest-wins lookup, two-page compaction, and write orders chosen so a power loss loses work rather than data; the format is the port's own and not Zephyr NVS.
Also implemented the Aliro provisioning backend over that store (storage/aliro_prov_kv.c): one key, a usable dev identity on every failure path, oversized records refused rather than parsed, and a factory reset that deletes the key rather than the store so OpenThread's SRP client key survives it.
Also started the BSP: board/time_freertos.c gives uptime from the software-extended FreeRTOS tick and the cycle counter and busy wait from RTC1's free-running counter (24-bit wrap carried in software, waits rounded up plus a guard tick so they are never short, a stopped counter fatal on first use rather than an unbounded spin), and board/fault_freertos.c logs and resets.
Remains, in the BSP: entropy, die temperature, the log sink, and flash arbitrated against radio timeslots; then the otPlatSettings adapter over the key-value store, and OpenThread crypto, still blocked on the crypto backend (the millisecond alarm, entropy, reset and assertions are done; the microsecond alarm is off by configuration) now that the pinned radio platform compiles and is started and serviced (the timestamper platform is not needed on nRF52833; its service layer asks for none of those symbols), prove nRF52833 BLE/OpenThread coexistence, then implement and validate the board, UWB, crypto, USB, boot, and DFU backends plus product orchestration.
Gates: preserve the hardware-in-loop Aliro BLE/URSK/Pre-POLL verdict, meet the DW3110 response-arm timing under concurrent BLE and Thread load, fit 512 KB flash and 128 KB RAM, and retain recoverable signed update behavior.
